# Durable Agent Activation

This tutorial uses `Jido.activate_agent/4` to start a durable Agent the same way on first use and on every later start. It also shows how the strict start functions differ, and which records `Jido.activate_agent/4` refuses to replace.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Why one operation

A caller with a stable ID often does not know whether the Agent has a record yet. Checking storage and then choosing a start function has a race, because another caller can create the record between the two steps. `Jido.activate_agent/4` closes that race with these steps:

1. If this instance already has a live activation for the Ref, it returns that process.
2. Otherwise the new server reads the record.
3. An active record is restored.
4. A `:not_found` read creates revision zero with compare-and-swap.
5. If that create conflicts, the server reads and restores the record that won.

Only `:not_found` leads to a create. A tombstone, an invalid record, and a storage error stop the activation instead.

## Set up an order Agent

An activation uses a `Jido.Agent.Ref`, so the instance needs a namespace. Build the Ref with `Jido.agent_ref/3`, which fills in that namespace.

```elixir
defmodule ActivationGuide.AddItem do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "activation_add_item",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{sku: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{sku: sku}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | items: [sku | state.items]}}
  end
end

defmodule ActivationGuide.Order do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "activation_order"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             items: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/guides/activation"
    route "order.add_item", ActivationGuide.AddItem, as: :add_item
  end
end

{:ok, _} =
  Jido.start_link(
    name: ActivationGuide.Jido,
    namespace: "guides/activation",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :activation_guide}
  )

{:ok, order_ref} = Jido.agent_ref(ActivationGuide.Jido, "order-1001")
order_ref
```

The cell returns `%Jido.Agent.Ref{id: "order-1001", namespace: "guides/activation", partition: nil}`. A Ref is an identifier. It holds no process and no credential.

## Activate for the first time

No record exists, so the server creates revision zero. `Jido.activate_agent/4` defaults to `persistence: :inherit`. The function can return before recovery finishes, so wait for readiness first.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order
alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

{:ok, server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order)
:ok = Server.await_ready(server)

{Server.agent(server).state, Server.snapshot(server).state_version}
```

The cell returns `{%{items: []}, 0}`.

## Send work through the Ref

The Ref functions on `Jido` resolve the process again for each call. Use them when you do not want to hold a PID. `Jido.resolve_agent/2` shows the current answer, which is `{:ok, pid}`, `{:error, :recovering}` while the server is not ready, or `{:error, :not_found}`.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order

{:ok, signal} = Order.add_item_signal(%{sku: "sku-red-mug"})
{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.call(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, signal)

{agent.state, Jido.resolve_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref) == {:ok, server}}
```

The cell returns `{%{items: ["sku-red-mug"]}, true}`.

## Activate again

A second call finds the live activation and returns the same process. Many concurrent callers also get one activation, because the instance Registry allows one server for each Ref.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order

{:ok, ^server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order)

results =
  1..20
  |> Enum.map(fn _caller ->
    Task.async(fn -> Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order) end)
  end)
  |> Task.await_many()

Enum.uniq(results) == [{:ok, server}]
```

The cell returns `true`.

Concurrent activation of a Ref that has no record, for example on different nodes that share a store, uses compare-and-swap on the create. One create wins and the other server restores the winning record.

## Restore after a stop

Stopping the server leaves the record in place. Calling `Jido.activate_agent/4` again restores it with the same state and revision.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order
alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

:ok = Jido.stop_agent_ref(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref)
{:error, :not_found} = Jido.resolve_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref)

{:ok, server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order)
:ok = Server.await_ready(server)

{Server.agent(server).state, Server.snapshot(server).state_version}
```

The cell returns `{%{items: ["sku-red-mug"]}, 1}`.

## Compare the strict functions

Two other functions keep narrower meanings. Use them when a wrong answer should fail instead of being corrected.

| Function | Mode | No record | Active record | Tombstone |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.activate_agent/4` | `:restore_or_create` | Creates revision 0 | Restores | Stops with `:deleted` |
| `Jido.start_agent_ref/4` with `persistence: :inherit` | `:create` | Creates revision 0 | Stops with `:conflict` | Stops with `:conflict` |
| `Jido.thaw/4` | `:restore` | Stops with `:not_found` | Restores | Stops with `:deleted` |

These reasons arrive after the start call, because the read happens in the new server. The helper below turns each outcome into a value. A fast failure can make the start itself return `{:error, reason}`. Otherwise `Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` returns `{:error, reason}`. Handle both forms. `{:error, :worker_unavailable}` and `{:error, :noproc}` mean that no reason was recorded, so read the record to learn why.

```elixir
defmodule ActivationGuide.Outcome do
  alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

  def of({:ok, pid}), do: Server.await_ready(pid)
  def of({:error, reason}), do: {:error, reason}
end
```

A running Agent rejects both strict functions with `{:error, {:already_started, pid}}`. Stop it first to see the storage rules.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.{Order, Outcome}

{:error, {:already_started, ^server}} =
  Jido.thaw(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-1001")

:ok = Jido.stop_agent_ref(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref)

create = Jido.start_agent_ref(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order, persistence: :inherit)
create_outcome = Outcome.of(create)

missing = Outcome.of(Jido.thaw(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-unknown"))

true = create_outcome != :ok
true = missing != :ok

{create_outcome, missing}
```

The strict create refused the existing record, and the strict restore refused to invent one. Neither changed storage, so the order still loads at revision 1.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order

{:ok, saved, 1} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent_with_revision(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-1001")

{:error, :not_found} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-unknown")

saved.state
```

## Refuse a deleted order

A tombstone is a saved decision, so activation does not reverse it. `Jido.delete_agent/4` writes the tombstone and requires a stopped Agent. Activation then stops and the same Ref stays deleted.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.{Order, Outcome}

:ok = Jido.delete_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order)

outcome = Outcome.of(Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_ref, Order))
true = outcome != :ok

{:error, :deleted} = Jido.Persistence.load_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-1001")
outcome
```

An invalid record, changed code, and a storage read error follow the same rule. The activation stops with its reason and creates nothing. Repair the data or the code, then activate again. [Deletion and Tombstones](deletion-and-tombstones.md) covers reuse of a deleted ID.

## Use a partition

A partition is part of the Ref, so the same ID in another partition is a separate record and a separate process.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order
alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

{:ok, eu_ref} = Jido.agent_ref(ActivationGuide.Jido, "order-1001", partition: "eu")
{:ok, eu_server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, eu_ref, Order)
:ok = Server.await_ready(eu_server)

{:ok, _saved} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-1001", partition: "eu")

{:error, :deleted} = Jido.Persistence.load_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, Order, "order-1001")

Server.agent(eu_server).state
```

The cell returns `%{items: []}`. The tombstone in the default partition did not affect this record.

## Clean up

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop(ActivationGuide.Jido)
```

## After BEAM loss

Activation is a call your application makes. Jido does not scan storage or find Agents to restart. Keep the IDs of durable Agents somewhere your application can read at boot, such as a table of open orders, and activate each one with the same namespace, store, and compatible code.

